  "content": {
    "id": "p27-q05-node",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "2",
    "prompt": "Two variations of the chain rule were covered above.\n\n• If $y$ is a function of $u$, and $u$ is a function of $x$, then $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{dy}{du}\\times\\dfrac{du}{dx}$.\n\n• If $h(x)=f(g(x))$, then $h'(x)=f'(g(x))\\times g'(x)$.\n\nShow that they are the same.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Set $u=g(x)$ and $y=f(u)=h(x)$. Then $\\dfrac{dy}{du}=f'(g(x))$ and $\\dfrac{du}{dx}=g'(x)$, giving $h'(x)=f'(g(x))g'(x)$.",
      "worked": "Let $u=g(x)$ and $y=f(u)=h(x)$. Then $\\dfrac{dy}{du}=f'(u)=f'(g(x))$ and $\\dfrac{du}{dx}=g'(x)$. Hence\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{dy}{dx}&=\\frac{dy}{du}\\frac{du}{dx}\\\\\\frac{dy}{dx}&=f'(g(x))g'(x)\\\\h'(x)&=f'(g(x))g'(x).\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 38
  },
